// Impure: mutates a variable that exists outside the render
let guest = 0;
function BadCup() {
  guest = guest + 1;                    // side effect during render
  return <h2>Tea cup for guest #{guest}</h2>;
}

// Pure: output depends only on props
function Cup({ guest }) {
  return <h2>Tea cup for guest #{guest}</h2>;
}

export default function TeaSet() {
  // Local mutation inside render is fine: the array is created here
  const cups = [];
  for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    cups.push(<Cup key={i} guest={i} />);
  }
  return (
    <>
      {cups}
      {/* Under StrictMode this shows #2, #4, #6: the double render exposes the bug */}
      <BadCup /><BadCup /><BadCup />
    </>
  );
}

concepts.body:
An **Effect** lets a component synchronize with a system outside React: a network connection, a browser API, a third-party widget, a subscription. It runs **after** React has committed the render to the screen:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const conn = createConnection(roomId);
  conn.connect();
  return () => conn.disconnect();   // cleanup
}, [roomId]);                        // dependencies
```

Think of it as "keep this external thing in sync with these values", not as lifecycle events. The **dependency array** lists every reactive value the effect reads (props, state, and anything computed from them). React re-runs the effect after a render where a dependency changed (compared with `Object.is`), calling the previous **cleanup** first. Without the array, it runs after every render; with `[]`, only on mount and cleanup on unmount.

Don't lie about dependencies; the `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` rule checks them. If an effect re-runs too often, change the code (move an object inside the effect, use an updater function) rather than omitting a dependency. For logic that should read the latest values without re-triggering the effect, React 19.2 adds `useEffectEvent`.

Cleanup must undo setup, because Strict Mode deliberately runs setup, cleanup, setup on mount to verify it.

**You might not need an Effect.** Effects are for external systems. Don't use one to:

- compute derived data (`useEffect(() => setFull(first + last))`); compute it during render;
- respond to a user event (send the POST in the submit handler, not in an effect watching a `submitted` flag);
- reset state when a prop changes; give the component a `key` instead.

Each unnecessary effect adds an extra render and a window where the UI shows stale values.

concepts.code:
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

function ChatRoom({ roomId }) {
  const [messages, setMessages] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const socket = new WebSocket(`wss://chat.example.com/rooms/${roomId}`);
    socket.onmessage = e => setMessages(m => [...m, e.data]); // updater: no dep on messages
    return () => socket.close();       // runs before the next setup and on unmount
  }, [roomId]);                        // reconnect only when the room changes

  return <ul>{messages.map((m, i) => <li key={i}>{m}</li>)}</ul>;
}

function WindowWidth() {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);
  useEffect(() => {
    const onResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);
    window.addEventListener("resize", onResize);
    return () => window.removeEventListener("resize", onResize);
  }, []);
  return <p>{width}px</p>;
}

export default function App() {
  const [room, setRoom] = useState("general");
  return <><button onClick={() => setRoom("random")}>Switch</button><ChatRoom roomId={room} /><WindowWidth /></>;
}

concepts.body:
A **ref** is a box whose `.current` you can read and write without causing a re-render:

```jsx
const intervalRef = useRef(null);
intervalRef.current = setInterval(tick, 1000);
```

Use refs for values that the rendered output doesn't depend on: timer ids, the previous value of something, an instance of a third-party library. If changing the value should update the screen, it must be state. Don't read or write `ref.current` during rendering (except lazy one-time initialisation); do it in handlers and effects, otherwise rendering becomes impure.

The most common use is accessing **DOM nodes**. Pass a ref object to a built-in element's `ref` attribute and React sets `.current` to the DOM node after it mounts (and back to `null` when it unmounts):

```jsx
const inputRef = useRef(null);
<input ref={inputRef} />
// in a handler: inputRef.current.focus();
```

In React 19, `ref` is an ordinary **prop for function components**: a child can accept `{ ref }` and forward it to an inner element, so `forwardRef` is no longer needed. You can also pass a **ref callback**, `ref={node => ...}`, which may now return a cleanup function, useful for attaching observers per node.

Use DOM refs for things React doesn't model: focus, scroll position, measuring, media playback, integrating a chart library. Don't use them to change content React manages (adding children, changing text); React will overwrite or crash on it.

A related escape hatch is the **portal**. `createPortal(children, domNode)` from `react-dom` renders children into a different DOM node, like `document.body`, while they stay in the same place in the React tree, so context and event bubbling still work. It's how modals, tooltips and toasts escape `overflow: hidden` containers.

concepts.code:
import { useRef, useState } from "react";
import { createPortal } from "react-dom";

// React 19: ref is a regular prop, no forwardRef needed
function TextField({ label, ref }) {
  return <label>{label} <input ref={ref} /></label>;
}

export default function Search() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);
  const renders = useRef(0);          // mutable, never triggers a render
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);

  return (
    <>
      <TextField label="Search" ref={inputRef} />
      <button onClick={() => inputRef.current.focus()}>Focus</button>
      <button onClick={() => { renders.current++; setOpen(true); }}>Help</button>
      {open &&
        createPortal(
          <div className="modal">
            <p>Type and press Enter.</p>
            <button onClick={() => setOpen(false)}>Close</button>
          </div>,
          document.body          // rendered here, but still a child in React's tree
        )}
    </>
  );
}

concepts.body:
When several components need the same stateful logic (subscribing to online status, reading window size, a debounced value), extract it into a **custom Hook**: a function whose name starts with `use` and that calls other Hooks.

```jsx
function useOnlineStatus() {
  const [isOnline, setIsOnline] = useState(navigator.onLine);
  useEffect(() => { /* subscribe to online/offline events */ }, []);
  return isOnline;
}
```

Any component can now write `const isOnline = useOnlineStatus();`.

Important properties:

- Custom Hooks share **logic, not state**. Each call gets its own independent state, exactly as if the component had called `useState` and `useEffect` itself.
- They follow the Rules of Hooks: call them at the top level, and only from components or other Hooks. The `use` prefix is what lets the linter (and the React Compiler) know the rules apply.
- They receive fresh arguments on every render, so they can react to changing props: `useChatRoom({ roomId })`.
- A function that doesn't call any Hooks shouldn't be named `useSomething`; make it a plain function.

Good custom Hooks are named after **what they do** for the component (`useOnlineStatus`, `useFormField`, `useMediaQuery`), hiding the effect underneath. Generic "lifecycle" wrappers like `useMount` tend to hide bad effect usage rather than help.

Custom Hooks are also a nice migration seam: if you later replace your hand-written effect with `useSyncExternalStore` or a data library, the components calling the Hook don't change.

concepts.code:
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

// Debounce any changing value
function useDebouncedValue(value, delay = 300) {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [value, delay]);
  return debounced;
}

function useLocalStorage(key, initial) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(() => {
    const saved = localStorage.getItem(key);
    return saved !== null ? JSON.parse(saved) : initial;
  });
  useEffect(() => localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value)), [key, value]);
  return [value, setValue];
}

export default function SearchBox() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useLocalStorage("query", "");
  const debounced = useDebouncedValue(query, 500);
  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <p>Searching for: {debounced}</p>
    </>
  );
}

