concepts.body:
As a component grows, state updates get scattered across many handlers. A **reducer** gathers them into one pure function:

```jsx
function reducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "added":   return [...state, { id: action.id, text: action.text, done: false }];
    case "deleted": return state.filter(t => t.id !== action.id);
    default: throw new Error("Unknown action: " + action.type);
  }
}

const [tasks, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialTasks);
dispatch({ type: "added", id: 3, text: "Write docs" });
```

Event handlers now describe **what happened** (`dispatch({ type: "deleted", id })`) and the reducer decides **how state changes**. The reducer receives the current state and the action and returns the next state.

Rules for reducers:

- They must be **pure**: no requests, timers or mutations; return new objects instead of modifying `state`. (Strict Mode calls them twice to check.)
- Each action describes one user interaction, even if it changes several fields. `"reset_form"` beats five `"set_field"` actions.
- Name actions in the past tense or as events (`"added"`, `"item_selected"`).

When to choose `useReducer` over `useState`: several pieces of state change together, the next state depends on the previous in non-trivial ways, or you want to unit-test the logic in isolation (a reducer is just a function). For a boolean or a single input, `useState` is simpler.

`dispatch` has a stable identity, so it's safe to pass deep down or put in context; combining a reducer with context is a lightweight app-state pattern. A third argument, `init`, computes the initial state lazily: `useReducer(reducer, arg, init)`.

concepts.code:
import { useReducer } from "react";

function cartReducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "added": {
      const existing = state.items.find(i => i.id === action.item.id);
      const items = existing
        ? state.items.map(i => (i.id === action.item.id ? { ...i, qty: i.qty + 1 } : i))
        : [...state.items, { ...action.item, qty: 1 }];
      return { ...state, items };
    }
    case "removed":
      return { ...state, items: state.items.filter(i => i.id !== action.id) };
    case "coupon_applied":
      return { ...state, discount: action.percent };
    default:
      throw new Error(`Unknown action ${action.type}`);
  }
}

export default function Cart() {
  const [cart, dispatch] = useReducer(cartReducer, { items: [], discount: 0 });
  const total = cart.items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price * i.qty, 0) * (1 - cart.discount / 100);
  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: "added", item: { id: 1, name: "Pen", price: 2 } })}>Add pen</button>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: "coupon_applied", percent: 10 })}>SAVE10</button>
      <p>{cart.items.length} lines, total {total.toFixed(2)}</p>
    </>
  );
}

concepts.body:
The basic client-side pattern fetches in an effect, keyed by the inputs:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  let ignore = false;
  fetchUser(id).then(u => { if (!ignore) setUser(u); });
  return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [id]);
```

The `ignore` flag matters. If `id` changes quickly, responses can arrive out of order; without the flag, a slow old response overwrites the new one (a **race condition**). An `AbortController` is the alternative: abort in the cleanup and ignore the `AbortError`. You also need loading and error states, which usually means a small state machine (`status: "loading" | "success" | "error"`).

Hand-written effect fetching has real limits: no caching (navigating back refetches), no deduplication, **waterfalls** when parent and child each fetch after rendering, and nothing on the first server render. In production code, use one of:

- A **data library** such as TanStack Query or SWR: `useQuery({ queryKey: ["user", id], queryFn })` gives caching, deduping, background refetch, retries and loading/error state.
- A **framework's data APIs**: route loaders in React Router or Server Components in Next.js load data before or during render, on the server, without waterfalls.
- React 19's **`use(promise)`** with **Suspense**: a component can read a promise created by a cache or a parent (or passed from a Server Component), suspending until it resolves. Don't create the promise inside the same component's render; it would be new every time.

Wrap the logic in a custom Hook (`useUser(id)`) so you can swap implementations later. Mutations (POST/PUT) don't belong in effects at all: run them in event handlers or Actions.

concepts.code:
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

function useJson(url) {
  const [state, setState] = useState({ status: "loading", data: null, error: null });

  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    setState({ status: "loading", data: null, error: null });

    (async () => {
      try {
        const res = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
        setState({ status: "success", data: await res.json(), error: null });
      } catch (error) {
        if (error.name !== "AbortError") setState({ status: "error", data: null, error });
      }
    })();

    return () => controller.abort();   // stale requests never win the race
  }, [url]);

  return state;
}

export default function User({ id }) {
  const { status, data, error } = useJson(`https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/${id}`);
  if (status === "loading") return <p>Loading...</p>;
  if (status === "error") return <p>Failed: {error.message}</p>;
  return <h2>{data.name}</h2>;
}

